Nope, I never noticed the gods get angry because I'm a thief.  I just don't
steal in that particular way.  In fact, unless I'm playing a Healer, I leave
Spazz (the dog) on level 1.

If I pray often enough (but not too often) I'm usually playing the Hand of
Elbereth pretty soon and I can't use a trained dog anyway.  My remark is
really about the change in strategy that seemed called for once I understood
what the gods could do for me.

For one thing, I can pray safely 100 turns earlier if I'm hungry enough to
be weak, and in this case I'm gauranteed to be returned to near satiety.
It turns out that this means I never really need food again for eating.

In fact, I use food only for sacrifices (except the occasional emergency
when a miscalculation gets the gods mad at me and I need a snack to survive).
Sacrifices will mollify the gods so they aren't angry with me.  If they are
not mad, then a sacrifice will reduce the waiting time for the next safe
time to pray (you get a feeling of reconciliation).  If it's safe to pray,
a sacrifice will bring mondo luck (you find a four-leaf clover). Only if
everything is perfect and luck maxed-out, will a sacrifice do nothing in
particular.  In this state, prayer has a very good chance of making you
the Hand, or giving you powers, or other neat stuff.

As soon as you are the Hand, you can't get into stores in the usual way
any more, so I don't count on this trick at all.  I generally do fine
without stores anyway.  With luck this high, every successful prayer is
gauranteed to solve at least one of your pressing problems (like turning
to stone from a cockatrice, being blind, etc) and will surely uncurse
anything weilded or worn.  So I don't need stores for armor, at least.

Since you tend to get lots of godly protection this way, I'm
frequently at a negative armor class when mother naked anyway.  I'm not
infrequently at -20 or so when suited up.  With that kind of protection,
my main worry is black D's, so I'm very eager to nuke all D's at the
first opportunity (the Hand doesn't need them for anything).

Usually, what I'm desperately looking for is the ring of TP control and
either a blindfold or a mirror.  A potion of blindness will do in a
pinch.  These are to deal with the medusa, which must not be looked upon.
A blindfold is best.  Use it on entering each level in the 20's and
you know whether to worry about the medusa or not.  If so, magic mapping
if you've got it, and otherwise carefully approach and beat on the old 8
while blind.

Using this approach, once I reach level 5 or so, I expect to be killed
only by system crashes, D's (if I haven't been able to genocide them)
or carelessness.


